1
00:00:00,460 --> 00:00:04,410
Two fifty-eight a.m. You're
paged: the site is down.

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:06,190
What broke first?

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:10,360
Three hours of syslog from
four hosts, on one timeline.

4
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:13,170
Zoom into the outage: five-oh-twos
from both web servers,

5
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:15,957
and right before them, the database.

6
00:00:16,297 --> 00:00:20,147
Pull out the database's own entries:
its disk filling for forty minutes,

7
00:00:20,197 --> 00:00:21,833
then no space left.

8
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:25,107
And at the start of the
climb: a backup job,

9
00:00:25,157 --> 00:00:27,564
writing to the database's own disk.

10
00:00:29,137 --> 00:00:31,967
Point your syslog at
OpenChronology, and next time,

11
00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:35,554
the timeline's already there. It's free.
